course: dictionary
lesson: 215
title: Where words come from
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Algonquian is a word for a large family of languages. Languages in one family are related, a bit like cousins. This family includes Cree, Ojibwe, Mi'kmaq and Blackfoot, which are Native American and First Nations languages of North America.
explain: Algonquian can also be an adjective, describing the peoples who speak these languages. Look at the words around it to see which job it does. In a sentence about languages, it names the family. In a sentence about people, it describes them.
explain: Many English words came from Algonquian languages. The example says, «Where a word comes from decides a lot: moose came from a Native American (Algonquian) language and mongoose from Marathi, a language of India, so neither has anything to do with goose.» The origin of a word tells you what it is related to.
fact: Algonquian is a language family: a group of related languages.
fact: As an adjective, Algonquian describes peoples who speak these languages. As a noun, it can name the language family.
fact: The Algonquian family includes Cree, Ojibwe, Mi'kmaq and Blackfoot.
fact: English words that came from Algonquian languages include moose, skunk, raccoon and moccasin.
fact: «Moose» came from an Algonquian language, but «mongoose» came from Marathi, so they are not related to «goose».
exercise: Which of these is NOT an Algonquian language? | *Marathi | Mi'kmaq | Ojibwe | Cree
exercise: What does Algonquian describe when it is used as an adjective? | A large city in Canada | A type of weather | *The peoples who speak these languages | A kind of food
exercise: «Moose came from a Native American (Algonquian) language.» Find and copy the word in brackets that names the language family. | American | Native | language | *Algonquian
test: Algonquian is best described as: | A kind of tree | A single word meaning goose | A town in India | *A large family of Native American and First Nations languages
test: Why does «mongoose» not become «mongeese»? | Because it is an Algonquian word | Because goose has no plural | *Because it came from Marathi, not from goose | Because it is a plural word
exam: Which English word came from an Algonquian language? | Goose | Mongoose | *Skunk | Lion
